# WHAT M-REBEL-LIBRARY SUPERSEDED, HELD TO THE TREE: the landing's final check,
# and the reading of a milestone page retired into it.
#
# The author's decisions of 2026-11-15 (`docs/roadmap/milestones/M-rebel-library.md`)
# replaced design.md §1.11's founding constraint: decision 9, *Heroes imposes no
# standard library and offers one that is optional*, with the Zen's law 18
# rewritten; decision 8, `core` no longer exists, every trace removed; decision
# 10, the decisions this supersedes declared invalidated in one register, living
# texts rewritten clean, and the sittings, journals and closed issues kept, each
# with its note. Panel 253's R1 (ratified 2026-11-19) made the register one
# `decision` issue. A sentence is landed in a dozen files by several lanes, and
# a file nobody re-read keeps the old one in silence, so the milestone's page
# asked a check of the net rather than a reading (its decision *the final
# check*). Four parts, each a label of `records`:
#
# 1. `records/sentence`: the new sentence stands in each of its homes, the
#    words each home must hold written down below (`homes`);
# 2. `records/retired`: no wording the decisions retired stands in a living
#    text, English or Italian (`RETIRED`);
# 3. `records/core`: `core` as a package stands in no living file (`CORE`);
# 4. `records/noted`: every record the register lists carries its note, and
#    names the register (`NOTED`, `REGISTER`).
#
# Parts 2 and 3 walk the project's files that are not records (the walk
# `suite_records.hero` hands in), less a retired milestone page and `EXEMPT`.
# Text is compared FOLDED (`folded`): lowercase, every run of white space one
# space, a line's leading `#` and `>` marks and every `*` dropped, and the HTML
# entities and curly quotes the site writes read as their characters, so a
# sentence wrapped across lines, in a comment, a quote or a page reads as one.
#
# And the milestone page retired into it: a row of the chain is a milestone the
# work owes or has closed, and on 2026-11-15 decision 4 enclosed
# M-core-packages and M-doc-generator in M-rebel-library, decision 23 of
# 2026-11-16 M-web-framework; their rows left the chain at the landing while
# their pages stayed, each the record of what it held, and `records/homes`
# accepts such a page by the line `retired_into` reads.
#
# design.md §1.11 is the section these sentences rewrite.

use cards
use report
use strings

# How the line a retired page carries opens: `**Retired YYYY-MM-DD into
# M-<name>**`, the day the author's decision retired it and the milestone that
# encloses it. Bold, at the start of a line, as the pages' dated notes are.
constant RETIRED: str
    "**Retired "

# Law 18 of the Zen, decision 9's words.
constant LAW_18: str
    "There is no standard library to obey; there is a Rebel to call, or not."

# CLAUDE.md § 13's line, the author's words (panel 253's spec-warden report).
constant CONTRACT_13: str
    "A standard library shipped with the compiler or the runtime, or one the compiler is written with; Rebel is a package a program takes or leaves."

# Decision 9's sentence, the words every English home holds.
constant SENTENCE: str
    "imposes no standard library"

# Its Italian, as the site's Italian edition says it: both words, since the
# translation's order is the translator's.
constant SENTENCE_IT: [str]
    [
        "non impone"
        "libreria standard"
    ]

# One home of the new sentence: the file, the heading that opens the section
# read ("" for the whole file), and every phrase it must hold.
record Home
    path: str
    within: str
    says: [str]

# Where the new sentence lives, a premise about the world written down: design.md
# §1.11, the Zen in `doctor.hero`, README.md, CLAUDE.md § 13, the ffi-pragmatist's
# charter, and the site's pages that said the old sentence or are to say the new
# one, both editions (the landing's site brief). A home that is not there is a
# failure, so a page moved without this list is told rather than skipped.
function homes() -> [Home]
    out: [Home] @= []
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "docs/design.md", within: "### 1.11", says: [SENTENCE]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "selfhost/cli/doctor.hero", within: "", says: [LAW_18]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "README.md", within: "", says: [SENTENCE]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "CLAUDE.md", within: "## 13.", says: [CONTRACT_13]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: ".claude/agents/ffi-pragmatist.md", within: "", says: [SENTENCE]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "site/src/html/project.html", within: "", says: [LAW_18]))
    out @ out.push(Home(path: "site/src/html/it/project.html", within: "", says: [LAW_18]))

    for page in ["start.html", "why.html", "why/not.html", "why/faq.html", "docs/packages.html"]
        out @ out.push(Home(path: "site/src/html/" + page, within: "", says: [SENTENCE]))
        out @ out.push(Home(path: "site/src/html/it/" + page, within: "", says: SENTENCE_IT))

    return out

# The wordings decisions 8 and 9 retired, folded: design.md §1.11's box and
# title, Part 2's bullet, the old law 18 and its first form, CLAUDE.md § 13's
# old line, the ffi-pragmatist's old charter, and the site's and the gallery's
# sentences in both editions. Each is long enough that the new sentence cannot
# hold it: `there is no standard library to obey` is not one of them.
constant RETIRED_WORDINGS: [str]
    [
        "out of scope, permanently"
        "do not accumulate convenience functions"
        "the founding constraint: there is no standard library"
        "that is the whole standard library"
        "there is no standard library; c is the man who sold the world"
        "there is no standard library, everything comes from c"
        "there is no standard library, permanently"
        "there is no standard library, on purpose"
        "there is no standard library."
        "a standard library. anything in design.md part 6"
        "panel judge for the founding constraint"
        "no standard library</span>"
        "una libreria standard non c'è"
        "non c'è una libreria standard"
        "niente libreria standard"
    ]

# `core` as a package, folded: its alias in a `use`, its folder in the index,
# its last version. An HTML page's `&lt;core&gt;/` folds to the first.
constant CORE: [str]
    [
        "<core>/"
        "packages/core"
        "core 0.6.0"
    ]

# What parts 2 and 3 leave out, each with its reason, each held to selecting a
# file so an exemption that reaches nothing is told (`records/exemptions`'
# rule): the seed is generated and changes at its next refresh (the landing
# brief), and this module quotes every wording it refuses.
constant EXEMPT: [str]
    [
        "seed/heroes.c"
        "tests/harness/superseded.hero"
    ]

# The records the register lists as carrying its note: four sittings, a
# journal, and the three milestone pages retired into M-rebel-library. Held
# both ways to the register's body once GitHub holds it; a record a later
# comment of the register adds joins this list by hand.
constant NOTED: [str]
    [
        "docs/panel/080-the-egg-that-cannot-hide.md"
        "docs/panel/234-the-core-packages-are-code-a-program-carries-in-gos-tree-under-core-a-thick-face-on-a-published-floor-a-card-that-shows-the-call-and-the-platforms-c-an-archive-beside-the-runtime.md"
        "docs/panel/235-dependencies-named-by-an-alias-in-angle-brackets-that-the-programs-catalogue-resolves-gos-modules-copied-whole-a-toml-catalogue-listing-every-package-and-no-fetched-code-run-at-build.md"
        "docs/panel/250-a-package-stays-placed-whole-since-no-placement-meets-the-compilers-ruler-and-feature-2315-waits-for-packages-core-does-not-hold.md"
        "docs/records/journal/075-package-manager.md"
        "docs/roadmap/milestones/M-core-packages.md"
        "docs/roadmap/milestones/M-doc-generator.md"
        "docs/roadmap/milestones/M-web-framework.md"
    ]

# The register's address. Until the coordinator files it, it is the
# placeholder every note writes, and the coordinator's one replacement over the
# tree writes the address here as in the notes. The placeholder is built from
# pieces where it is compared (`placeholder`), so that replacement cannot
# rewrite the comparison too.
constant REGISTER: str
    "https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/issues/2610"

function placeholder() -> str
    return "<" + "register" + ">"

# How a record's note opens: invalidated, in part or whole, or retired.
constant NOTE_OPENINGS: [str]
    [
        "**Invalidated"
        "**Retired "
    ]

# The line in the register's body that lists the records carrying its note.
constant CARRYING: str
    "carrying the note**"

# What GitHub answered of the issues, as this check reads it: whether it was
# asked, why not where it was not, and each issue's body by its number. The
# records suite reads the listing once and hands this in, so this module, and
# a run of its own tests, carries none of the transport.
record Answered
    asked: bool
    broken: bool
    why: str
    bodies: {i64: str}

# The four parts over the walk: `present` every path git names as the
# project's, `open_text` those of them that are not records, generated or
# closed issues.
function check(@r: report.Report, present: [str], open_text: [str], github: Answered)
    homes_part(@r, present: present)
    walked_parts(@r, present: present, open_text: open_text)
    noted_part(@r, present: present, github: github)

# Part 1.
function homes_part(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    offences: [str] @= []

    for home in homes()
        text = read_file(home.path)

        if text.is_err() || !among(home.path, present)
            offences @ offences.push("    " + home.path + ": not there")
            continue

        for one in missing_in(text: text.must(), home: home)
            offences @ offences.push("    " + home.path + ": " + one)

    if offences.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "records/sentence",
        detail: "  a home of the new sentence (decision 9, panel 253's R1) does not hold it:\n" + offences.join("\n") + "\n  write it there in these words, or move the home in tests/harness/superseded.hero's `homes` with the page"
    )

# What `home` does not hold of its phrases in `text`, each told.
function missing_in(text: str, home: Home) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []
    read: str @= text

    if home.within != ""
        part = section(text: text, heading: home.within)

        if part.is_err()
            out @ out.push("no section opens `" + home.within + "`")
            return out
        read @ part.must()
    held = folded(read)

    for phrase in home.says
        if !strings.contains(s: held, substr: folded(phrase))
            where: str @= ""

            if home.within != ""
                where @ " in `" + home.within + "`"
            out @ out.push("does not say `" + phrase + "`" + where)

    return out

# The section of `text` that opens at the line beginning `heading`, up to the
# next heading of its level or above, or why there is none.
function section(text: str, heading: str) -> str?
    level = marks(heading)
    rows = strings.lines(text)
    out: [str] @= []
    inside: bool @= false

    for row in rows
        if inside && marks(row) > 0 && marks(row) <= level
            break

        if !inside && strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: heading)
            inside @ true

        if inside
            out @ out.push(row)

    if !inside
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "no line opens `" + heading + "`")
    return ok(out.join("\n"))

# How many `#` open a markdown heading's line, 0 for any other line.
function marks(row: str) -> i64
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < row.len() && row[at] == '#'
        at @ at + 1

    if at > 0 && at < row.len() && row[at] == ' '
        return at
    return 0

# Parts 2 and 3, one pass over the files that are not records.
function walked_parts(@r: report.Report, present: [str], open_text: [str])
    retired: [str] @= []
    cored: [str] @= []

    for path in open_text
        if among(path, EXEMPT)
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        raw = text.must()

        if strings.has_prefix(s: path, prefix: "docs/roadmap/milestones/") && !retired_into(raw).is_err()
            continue

        if might_retire(raw)
            for one in retired_in(raw)
                retired @ retired.push("    " + path + ": `" + one + "`")

        if strings.contains(s: raw, substr: "core")
            for one in core_in(raw)
                cored @ cored.push("    " + path + ": `" + one + "`")

    for path in EXEMPT
        if !among(path, present)
            retired @ retired.push("    " + path + ": an exemption of this check that selects no file")

    tell(
        @r,
        label: "records/retired",
        found: retired,
        why: "  a wording decisions 8 and 9 retired stands in a living text (decision 10: living texts are rewritten clean; the old words live in the register):"
    )
    tell(
        @r,
        label: "records/core",
        found: cored,
        why: "  `core` as a package stands in a living file (decision 8: every trace removed; seed/heroes.c and the records are exempt):"
    )

# A pass or one failure listing what `found` holds.
function tell(@r: report.Report, label: str, found: [str], why: str)
    if found.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
    else
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: why + "\n" + found.join("\n"))

# Whether `text` holds a word every retired wording holds one of, read before
# a file is folded, so the walk folds only the few that might hold one. Each
# piece leaves out its first letter, whose case varies.
function might_retire(text: str) -> bool
    for piece in ["ibrar", "ibrer", "onvenience", "ermanently", "ounding"]
        if strings.contains(s: text, substr: piece)
            return true

    return false

# The retired wordings `text` holds, folded.
function retired_in(text: str) -> [str]
    return found_in(text: folded(text), needles: RETIRED_WORDINGS)

# The spellings of `core` as a package `text` holds, folded.
function core_in(text: str) -> [str]
    return found_in(text: folded(text), needles: CORE)

function found_in(text: str, needles: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for needle in needles
        if strings.contains(s: text, substr: needle)
            out @ out.push(needle)

    return out

# Part 4.
function noted_part(@r: report.Report, present: [str], github: Answered)
    offences: [str] @= []
    filed = REGISTER != placeholder()

    if !filed
        offences @ offences.push(
            "    the register is not filed: its address is still `" + placeholder() + "` here and in every note; file it and write its address in place of the placeholder over the tree"
        )
    else if !cards.is_issue_url(REGISTER)
        offences @ offences.push("    `" + REGISTER + "` is not an issue's address")

    for path in NOTED
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err() || !among(path, present)
            offences @ offences.push("    " + path + ": not there")
            continue
        why = note_offence(text: text.must(), register: REGISTER)

        if why != ""
            offences @ offences.push("    " + path + ": " + why)

    if filed && cards.is_issue_url(REGISTER)
        if github.broken
            offences @ offences.push("    GitHub's answer could not be read: " + github.why)
        else if !github.asked
            report.keep_skip(
                @r,
                label: "records/noted",
                detail: "  GitHub could not be asked, so the register's body was not held to NOTED: " + github.why
            )
        else
            for one in against_register(bodies: github.bodies, register: REGISTER)
                offences @ offences.push("    " + one)

    if offences.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "records/noted",
        detail: "  a record the register lists does not carry its note (decision 10, panel 253's R1):\n" + offences.join("\n")
    )

# Why `text`, a record the register lists, does not carry its note: a line
# opening one of `NOTE_OPENINGS`, and the register's address from it on; ""
# where it does.
function note_offence(text: str, register: str) -> str
    rows = strings.lines(text)
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < rows.len()
        for opening in NOTE_OPENINGS
            if strings.has_prefix(s: rows[at], prefix: opening)
                rest: [str] @= []
                i: i64 @= at

                while i < rows.len()
                    rest @ rest.push(rows[i])
                    i @ i + 1

                if strings.contains(s: rest.join("\n"), substr: register)
                    return ""

        at @ at + 1

    return "no note opening `**Invalidated` or `**Retired ` names the register, `" + register + "`"

# The register's body against `NOTED`, both ways: what it lists as carrying its
# note and this module does not hold, and the reverse.
function against_register(bodies: {i64: str}, register: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []
    number = strings.to_number(strings.base_name(register))

    if number.is_err()
        out @ out.push("the register's address ends in no number: " + register)
        return out
    body = bodies[number.must()]

    if body.is_err()
        out @ out.push("the register, " + register + ", is not among the issues GitHub returned")
        return out
    listed = noted_in_register(body.must())

    for path in listed
        if !among(path, NOTED)
            out @ out.push(path + ": the register lists it and NOTED does not")

    for path in NOTED
        if !among(path, listed)
            out @ out.push(path + ": NOTED holds it and the register does not list it")

    return out

# The records a register's body lists as carrying its note: the code spans
# naming a `.md` file on each line that holds `CARRYING`.
function noted_in_register(body: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for row in strings.lines(body)
        if !strings.contains(s: row, substr: CARRYING)
            continue
        parts = strings.split_on(text: row, byte: '`')
        at: i64 @= 1

        while at < parts.len()
            if strings.has_suffix(s: parts[at], suffix: ".md") && !among(parts[at], out)
                out @ out.push(parts[at])
            at @ at + 2

    return out

# `text` folded for comparison: lowercase ASCII, every run of white space one
# space, a line's leading `#` and `>` marks and every `*` dropped, and the
# entities and curly quotes `ENTITIES` names read as what they stand for. Only
# ASCII bytes and whole characters move, so the answer is text whenever `text`
# is.
function folded(text: str) -> str
    out: [u8] @= []
    at: i64 @= 0
    line_start: bool @= true
    owed_space: bool @= false

    while at < text.len()
        b = text[at]

        if b == ' ' || b == '\t' || b == '\n' || b == '\r'
            if b == '\n'
                line_start @ true
            owed_space @ out.len() > 0
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if line_start && (b == '#' || b == '>')
            at @ at + 1
            continue
        line_start @ false

        if b == '*'
            at @ at + 1
            continue
        kept: u8 @= b
        width: i64 @= 1
        swapped = entity_at(text: text, at: at)

        if swapped >= 0
            width @ ENTITIES[swapped].len()
            kept @ ENTITY_BYTES[swapped]

        if kept == ' '
            owed_space @ out.len() > 0
            at @ at + width
            continue

        if owed_space
            out @ out.push(' ')
            owed_space @ false

        if kept >= 'A' && kept <= 'Z'
            kept @ kept + 32
        out @ out.push(kept)
        at @ at + width

    return out.validated_bytes().default("")

# The entities and quotes `folded` reads as one ASCII byte, and the byte each
# stands for, at the same index.
constant ENTITIES: [str]
    [
        "&rsquo;"
        "&lsquo;"
        "&#39;"
        "&apos;"
        "’"
        "‘"
        "&lt;"
        "&gt;"
        "&amp;"
        "&nbsp;"
        "&quot;"
    ]

constant ENTITY_BYTES: [u8]
    [
        '\''
        '\''
        '\''
        '\''
        '\''
        '\''
        '<'
        '>'
        '&'
        ' '
        '"'
    ]

# The index in `ENTITIES` of the one that stands at `at`, or -1.
function entity_at(text: str, at: i64) -> i64
    i: i64 @= 0

    while i < ENTITIES.len()
        one = ENTITIES[i]

        if at + one.len() <= text.len() && strings.holds_at(s: text, part: one, at: at)
            return i
        i @ i + 1

    return -1

function among(word: str, words: [str]) -> bool
    for one in words
        if one == word
            return true

    return false

# The milestone a retired page says it went into, read off its first line of
# the shape `RETIRED` opens, or why the page says none.
function retired_into(text: str) -> str?
    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if !strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: RETIRED)
            continue
        rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: RETIRED)

        # `day_of` reads ten ASCII bytes before it answers, so the cut at ten
        # stands between characters.
        if cards.day_of(rest).is_err()
            continue
        after = rest.slice(from: 10, to: rest.len())

        if !strings.has_prefix(s: after, prefix: " into ")
            continue
        named = strings.without_prefix(text: after, prefix: " into ")
        end = strings.index_of(text: named, needle: "**")

        if end.is_err()
            continue
        name = named.slice(from: 0, to: end.must())

        if cards.is_milestone_name(name)
            return ok(name)

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no line opens `**Retired YYYY-MM-DD into M-<name>**`")

## Tests

test "a retired page names the milestone that encloses it, on a dated line of its own"
    page = "# M-core-packages\n\nWhat it held.\n\n**Retired 2026-11-15 into M-rebel-library**, by the author's decision 4.\n"
    assert retired_into(page).must() == "M-rebel-library"
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-11-16 into M-rebel-packages-manager**\n").must() == "M-rebel-packages-manager"

test "a line that only resembles the retirement names nothing"
    assert retired_into("# M-doc-generator\n\nNothing retired here.\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("  **Retired 2026-11-15 into M-rebel-library**\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-13-15 into M-rebel-library**\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-11-15 to M-rebel-library**\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-11-15 into M-Rebel**\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-11-15 into M-rebel-library\n").is_err()
    assert retired_into("**Retired 2026-11-15 into rebel**\n").is_err()

test "a text is folded: case, white space, a line's marks, emphasis, entities and curly quotes"
    assert folded("  There  is\n> **no**\tstandard\n# library ") == "there is no standard library"
    assert folded("Non c&rsquo;è una libreria standard") == "non c'è una libreria standard"
    assert folded("Non c’è") == "non c'è"
    assert folded("use &lt;core&gt;/strings") == "use <core>/strings"
    assert folded("a&nbsp;b") == "a b"
    assert folded("") == ""

test "part 1 fires on a home that lacks the sentence, and passes one that wraps it"
    home = Home(path: "README.md", within: "", says: [SENTENCE])
    assert missing_in(text: "Heroes imposes no\nstandard library, and offers Rebel.", home: home).len() == 0
    assert missing_in(text: "There is no standard library.", home: home).len() == 1
    law = Home(path: "selfhost/cli/doctor.hero", within: "", says: [LAW_18])
    assert missing_in(text: "18. There is no standard library to obey; there is a Rebel to call, or not.\\n", home: law).len() == 0
    assert missing_in(text: "18. There is no standard library; C is the man who sold the world.\\n", home: law).len() == 1

test "part 1 reads a home's section alone, and tells a section that is not there"
    home = Home(path: "docs/design.md", within: "### 1.11", says: [SENTENCE])
    design = "### 1.10 Before\n\nHeroes imposes no standard library.\n\n### 1.11 The constraint\n\nIt ships a runtime.\n\n### 1.12 After\n"
    assert missing_in(text: design, home: home).len() == 1
    moved = "### 1.11 The constraint\n\nHeroes imposes no standard library.\n\n#### 1.11.1 Deeper\n\nstill inside\n\n## Part 2\n"
    assert missing_in(text: moved, home: home).len() == 0
    assert section(text: moved, heading: "### 1.11").must() == "### 1.11 The constraint\n\nHeroes imposes no standard library.\n\n#### 1.11.1 Deeper\n\nstill inside\n"
    assert missing_in(text: "## Part 1\n", home: home) == ["no section opens `### 1.11`"]

test "part 2 fires on every retired wording, English and Italian, and not on the new sentence"
    assert retired_in("Writing a broad standard library is out of\nscope, permanently.") == ["out of scope, permanently"]
    assert retired_in("18. There is no standard library; C is the man who sold the world.") == ["there is no standard library; c is the man who sold the world"]
    assert retired_in("<dd>Una libreria standard non c&rsquo;è, di proposito") == ["una libreria standard non c'è"]
    assert retired_in("<span class=\"when\">Niente libreria standard</span>") == ["niente libreria standard"]
    assert retired_in("18. There is no standard library to obey; there is a Rebel to call, or not.").len() == 0
    assert retired_in("Heroes imposes no standard library and offers one that is optional.").len() == 0
    assert might_retire("a Library") && might_retire("permanently") && !might_retire("nothing here")

test "part 3 fires on core as a package in every spelling, and not on a word that holds core"
    assert core_in("use <core>/strings") == ["<core>/"]
    assert core_in("<code>use &lt;core&gt;/strings</code>") == ["<core>/"]
    assert core_in("read from packages/core/0.6.0/list") == ["packages/core"]
    assert core_in("added core\n0.6.0") == ["core 0.6.0"]
    assert core_in("the score of core_strings and a `core` the record names").len() == 0

test "part 4 fires on a record with no note or a note that names no register"
    register = "https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/issues/1"
    noted = "the sitting\n\n**Invalidated in part by M-rebel-library, 2026-11-19**: R1 no longer holds.\nEntry 7 of the register, " + register + ".\n"
    assert note_offence(text: noted, register: register) == ""
    assert note_offence(text: "**Retired 2026-11-15 into M-rebel-library**, the register " + register + ".\n", register: register) == ""
    assert note_offence(text: "the sitting, untouched, " + register + "\n", register: register) != ""
    assert note_offence(text: "**Invalidated in part by M-rebel-library, 2026-11-19**: R1.\n", register: register) != ""

test "part 4 reads the records a register lists from its carrying lines alone"
    body = "- **Records carrying the note**: `docs/panel/234-x.md`; `docs/records/journal/075-y.md`.\n- **Also**: `docs/roadmap/milestones/M-package-index.md`.\n- **Closed issue carrying the note**: 2403.\n"
    assert noted_in_register(body) == ["docs/panel/234-x.md", "docs/records/journal/075-y.md"]

test "part 4 holds the register's list and NOTED to each other, both ways"
    whole: [str] @= []

    for path in NOTED
        whole @ whole.push("`" + path + "`")

    body = "- **Records carrying the note**: " + whole.join(", ") + ".\n"
    bodies: {i64: str} @= {}
    bodies[7] @ body
    register = "https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/issues/7"
    assert against_register(bodies: bodies, register: register).len() == 0
    bodies[7] @ body + "- **Record carrying the note**: `docs/panel/001-x.md`.\n"
    assert against_register(bodies: bodies, register: register) == ["docs/panel/001-x.md: the register lists it and NOTED does not"]
    bodies[7] @ "- **Record carrying the note**: `" + NOTED[0] + "`.\n"
    assert against_register(bodies: bodies, register: register).len() == NOTED.len() - 1
    assert against_register(bodies: bodies, register: "https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/issues/8") == ["the register, https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/issues/8, is not among the issues GitHub returned"]

test "the register is the placeholder until it is filed, and then an issue's address"
    assert placeholder() == "<" + "register>"
    assert REGISTER == placeholder() || cards.is_issue_url(REGISTER)

test "every exemption, home and noted record is a path of the tree as written, never a glob"
    for path in EXEMPT
        assert !strings.contains(s: path, substr: "*")

    for path in NOTED
        assert strings.has_suffix(s: path, suffix: ".md")

    for home in homes()
        assert home.says.len() > 0
